<urn:usf:cli-decision:restore-chain-operator-driven:1> <urn:usf:predicate:forCommand> "restore" <urn:usf:graph:foundation> .
<urn:usf:cli-decision:restore-chain-operator-driven:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "declining-to-register-the-chain-is-not-a-claim-that-the-chain-is-rare-it-was-run-more-than-twenty-times" <urn:usf:graph:foundation> .
<urn:usf:cli-decision:restore-chain-operator-driven:1> <urn:usf:predicate:retirementReason> "The stale-completion restoration chain will not become a single command. Every step of it already is one: evidence, prove, publish verified twice then applied, realise no-change, one check per graph-required validation, completion evidence, completion proof and a second twice-verified publish. The chain requires the operator to read back the prior digest and the stable result between steps, and a command that ran all eight in one invocation would mint two publications while hiding exactly the read-backs that make the sequence safe. What was missing was never a command: it was the documented order and its traps, which now live in the operator skill. The lane projection, which is pure observation with no authority effect, is registered instead as usf graph lanes." <urn:usf:graph:foundation> .
<urn:usf:cli-decision:restore-chain-operator-driven:1> <urn:usf:predicate:type> <urn:usf:type:RegisteredCommandRetirement> <urn:usf:graph:foundation> .
